Moth checks arithmetic instead of quietly wrapping or producing invalid
floating-point values.

These operations can fail:

- overflowing an integer type
- dividing by zero
- using an invalid integer exponent
- producing `NaN` or infinity

A function with an `Error!` return can pass that failure through its normal
error path:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    divide |left Int, right Int| -> Float, Error!:
        return left / right
    ;
]

Checked numeric failure is implicit built-in failure, not wrapping or an implicit
panic. A private function without an error slot can pass it through internal calls
in the same module without postfix `!`. A custom error return needs local recovery
or explicit mapping. An exported function without an error slot must handle it.
Entry code runs through `start` with `Error!`.

Use `catch` to recover the complete selected expression, including arithmetic
and failures inside call arguments or cast operands:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    total = left * right + offset catch then 0
]

The handler can also receive builtin `Error!` from calls in that expression.
See @../errors/#catch-and-recovery (Catch and recovery) for placement rules.

A failure the compiler can prove in advance is reported during compilation,
even inside `Error!` or an expression with `catch`.

Each operation checks its own result type rather than the storage width of its
operands. Fixed-width arithmetic typing and numeric constant folding are
implemented in the frontend/HIR. HTML-JS and experimental HTML-Wasm execute
checked runtime arithmetic, including fixed-width and trap-mode Wasm checks.
HTML-JS executes `Dec` arithmetic with exact coefficients and per-operation
half-even rounding at the result scale. Wasm rejects reachable `Dec` values
and recoverable numeric failures. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.
